The Codex consultation was not sent to completion. It was still in `turn.started` (i.e., Codex hadn't produced any output) 1 minute 21 seconds after launch, which exceeded the 1-minute override timeout and the staleness threshold, and no interactive `AskUserQuestion` tool was available in this session to ask whether to keep waiting — so per the procedure I stopped it rather than let it run unattended.

No claims were returned, so I have nothing from Codex to present or evaluate.

As for the underlying question, based on my own reading of `src/user.js`:

```js
export async function fetchUser(id, { retries = 2 } = {}) {
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= retries; attempt++) {
    try {
      return await api.get(`/users/${id}`, { timeout: 2000 });
    } catch (err) {
      if (err.name === "TimeoutError") {
        continue;
      }
      throw err;
    }
  }
  return {};
}
```

The empty object is returned deliberately, by design of the loop: on a `TimeoutError`, the `catch` block just does `continue` and retries; any other error is re-thrown immediately. If every attempt (the initial call plus `retries` retries — 3 attempts by default) times out, the loop falls through without ever hitting `return` or `throw`, and execution reaches `return {};` at the end of the function. So a timeout is swallowed silently after exhausting retries, and the caller gets `{}` indistinguishable from "no error occurred" — there's no way for the caller to tell this apart from a legitimately empty user record.

This is my own analysis, not Codex's (the consultation didn't complete) — if you want Codex's independent take, we can retry the consultation, possibly with a longer timeout.

